    "result_text": "All 36 tests pass, including the new Cp26 test. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `OwnerRestControllerV1.normalizeTelephone`.\n\n**Change** (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):\n\nAfter the existing general \"8\u201315 digits\" check, the code now also validates the national-number length against the country code via a new helper `hasValidNationalLength(String digits)`:\n- `+61` (digits starting with `61`) \u2192 requires exactly **9** national digits\n- `+1` (digits starting with `1`) \u2192 requires exactly **10** national digits\n- Any other country code (e.g. `+64`) remains bound only by the overall 8\u201315 digit limit\n\nA wrong length throws `InvalidTelephoneException`, which the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` maps to **400 Bad Request**.\n\nThe two country-code prefixes are unambiguous (`61` starts with `6`, not `1`), so they never collide. I verified this preserves all existing behavior: `+61 412 345 678` (9 digits) and the default `04XXXXXXXX` seed still pass, `+64 21 123 456` (8 national digits) stays valid under the lenient path, and the new `+61 123` case is correctly rejected. The `toE164OrNull` duplicate-comparison path already swallows `InvalidTelephoneException`, so legacy seed numbers of other lengths simply don't match rather than causing errors.\n\nFull suite: **36 tests, 0 failures**.",
